# Manager environment. Copy to manager/.env and edit, or to manager/.env.<name>
# for a host deploy/deploy.sh --profile=<name> deploys. A deploy sends that one
# file, and the host keeps it as manager/.env.
# Loaded by docker compose for ${VAR} interpolation AND injected into the
# postgres + api containers via `env_file:` in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Two run modes:
#   * `pnpm dev` (host) — needs DATABASE_URL + MANAGER_PORT (+ LOG_LEVEL)
#   * `docker compose up` (postgres, api and web) additionally uses
#     POSTGRES_PASSWORD.

# --- Postgres ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Used by the postgres container (POSTGRES_PASSWORD) and embedded into
# DATABASE_URL below. Any string; lives only inside this compose project.
# The value below is public, so deploy/deploy.sh refuses it unless
# --allow-sample-secrets says the install is a test one. Generate one with
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pwd

# --- Manager API ------------------------------------------------------------
# Required. Connection string the running app uses to reach Postgres.
#   - Host mode (`pnpm dev` against the dockerized postgres that `pnpm database:start`
#     publishes on localhost, at MANAGER_DEV_PG_PORT, 5432 unless set):
#       postgres://manager:<password>@localhost:5432/manager
#   - Docker mode (the `api` service): the compose file overrides this to
#       postgres://manager:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/manager
#     so the value below is only used in host mode. Keep them in sync.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://manager:pwd@localhost:5432/manager

# Optional. Host port the manager API listens on. Default: 9876.
MANAGER_PORT=9876

# Optional, server deployments only (docker-compose.yml). The host port the
# console is published on. Default: 8080.
# WEB_PORT=8080

# Optional, server deployments only (docker-compose.yml). The host address the
# console's web port, WEB_PORT, is published on. Default: 127.0.0.1, where the
# host's edge and an ssh tunnel reach it. Docker publishes past a host firewall
# such as ufw, so name another address only where something else stands in
# front of it.
# MANAGER_WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Optional. The address the manager API binds to. Default: 0.0.0.0, every
# interface, which is what the api container needs for the web container to
# reach it. Narrow it to 127.0.0.1 when the manager runs on the host and the
# port should answer nothing but the loopback.
# MANAGER_HOST=

# Optional. Public address (IP or DNS name) clients use to reach deployed
# components. deploy.sh sets this automatically from the server's host IP.
# If unset, the manager falls back to localhost, and component URLs then work
# only on this machine. It is also the public ingest address the web2 admin is
# given for a stage on this host that sets none of its own.
PUBLIC_HOST=

# Optional. The address the manager reaches a locally published Bee API on.
# Default: host.docker.internal inside the api container, 127.0.0.1 outside
# it. Set it to the stack's *_API_BIND address when that is not the bridge.
# Since 2026-09-17 it is also the host every local ABR pool string carries,
# see deploy/README.md.
# The stack's own deploy binds each Bee API to the host's Docker bridge
# address wherever nothing names a *_API_BIND, which is what this resolves to
# on a Linux host. A deployment on another host is dialled over the network,
# so the manager writes 0.0.0.0 into each of its Bee binds and listen
# addresses that nothing names, and that host's firewall decides who reaches it.
# A host name or an IPv4 address and nothing more: no scheme, no path, no
# port, no spaces, and no IPv6 address yet, since the manager writes this host
# into its addresses without brackets. A value of any other shape stops the
# manager at startup with a message naming this variable, rather than reading
# as an uploader or a Bee node that does not answer.
# BEE_LOCAL_HOST=

# Optional. The name this manager instance goes by, such as my-instance, which
# the sidebar shows under "Streaming Infra" beside the build it runs. At most 40
# letters, digits, dots, dashes or underscores. Any other value stops the
# manager at startup. Default: none, and the sidebar shows the build alone.
# MANAGER_INSTANCE_NAME=

# Optional. Log level: trace | debug | info | warn | error. Default: info.
# Applied by the api from startup. Each level writes its own lines and those of
# every level after it: error is failures alone, warn adds what an operator
# should look at, info adds what the manager did, and debug adds each read
# that failed and was answered for anyway (a metric, a disk usage, an uploader
# health address, an SRS log, an ssh alias), which repeat on every poll. trace
# writes what debug does. Any other value stops the manager at startup. The
# command line, node dist/cli.js, writes at info whatever this says.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional. Whether the web2 admin link on the Manager settings page may be
# plain http to another host than this one: true or false. Default: false.
# Every push to the link carries the admin's registrar token and each stage's
# SRT passphrase, so with this off the manager takes plain http only to its own
# host: a loopback address, host.docker.internal or the bridge address it
# resolves to, or a name that resolves into a Docker network of the api
# container, such as the admin's compose service name. Anything else needs
# https, and a save of it is refused. A link saved in plain http to another host
# before this rule is sent nothing until it is given https or this is set: its
# pushes say refused-plain-http and Test connection plain-http-refused. Set it
# to true only for a test setup. Any other value stops the manager at startup.
# ADMIN_LINK_ALLOW_PLAIN_HTTP=false

# Optional. The bearer token the web2 admin presents to the funding API under
# /api/admin-funding, which lists every stage's nodes and their wallets so the
# admin can fund them from the brand wallet. Unset, the funding API is off and
# every path under it answers 404. 32 characters or more with no space inside,
# for example from `openssl rand -hex 32`; anything shorter stops the manager at
# startup. The admin's MANAGER_FUNDING_TOKEN holds the same value. Only that
# API takes it: every other route refuses a bearer, and the funding API refuses
# a session cookie. It is never logged.
# FUNDING_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. The chain endpoint the funding API reads balances, nonces, fees
# and receipts through, and broadcasts the web2 admin's transfers to. Unset, it
# is BEE_RPC_ENDPOINT, and with neither the funding API's chain routes answer
# 502 chain_unreachable. Chequebook transfers read the chain through it too, see
# "Chequebook transfers" below. An http(s) URL like BEE_RPC_ENDPOINT; it can
# carry a provider key, so it is never logged or answered. A malformed value
# stops the manager at startup.
# FUNDING_RPC_URL=

# Optional. The chain endpoint this manager offers its Bee nodes. Every node
# created from the wizard is offered it first, and a node takes the stack's own
# public RPC only when nothing here is set or the operator chooses otherwise.
# A malformed value stops the manager at startup rather than reverting to the
# stack's.
#
# The safe shape is an address that carries no key, such as a proxy on this host
# that holds the key and forwards. A URL with a key in its path works, and the
# manager answers only its host to a page, takes it out of the container logs it
# serves and out of the deploy output it stores, and logs only its host itself.
# But the Bee node prints the address it was started with into its own container
# log, which docker logs shows to anyone with docker access on the host it runs
# on, and no manager code can prevent that.
#
# An endpoint URL that carries a key is a secret either way: set it in the
# host's environment rather than writing it in this file. The host on its own is not nothing: a provider that gives an account its
# own subdomain is named by the host alone, which is what a page is shown.
#
# Chequebook transfers read the chain through this address when
# CHEQUEBOOK_RPC_ENDPOINTS names no endpoint for their chain and FUNDING_RPC_URL
# is unset, see "Chequebook transfers" below.
# BEE_RPC_ENDPOINT=

# --- Chequebook transfers ---------------------------------------------------
# Fill chequebook and Withdraw reach a node through its Bee API, at the address
# and port its deployment recorded, as stamp top-ups do. No Docker connection
# and no ssh Host block is needed, on this host or any other. A transfer reads
# the chain through the first of these that is set: CHEQUEBOOK_RPC_ENDPOINTS for
# its chain, then FUNDING_RPC_URL, then BEE_RPC_ENDPOINT, each checked against
# the node's chain before use. With none set, every transfer is refused with
# chain_endpoint_missing. Changing one takes a restart. See "Funding a
# chequebook on a new host" in README.md.
#
# Per chain id (1, 100 or 11155111), an http or https endpoint every transfer on
# that chain reads through, ahead of FUNDING_RPC_URL and BEE_RPC_ENDPOINT. It
# can carry a key, so keep a real one in the host's environment rather than in
# this file.
# CHEQUEBOOK_RPC_ENDPOINTS={"100":"https://rpc.example.invalid"}

# Optional. How little BZZ a deployment's own bee node should hold available in
# its chequebook. Default: 0.5. The chequebook pays the peers that forward this
# node's uploads. Below the floor the manager warns in its log and on the
# deployment page and still starts the uploader, and
# that node's uploads stall until the chequebook is filled. A decimal amount
# above zero with at most 16 decimal places. A malformed value stops the manager
# at startup rather than reverting to the default, because this one number is
# what the UI shows and what the start warning quotes.
CHEQUEBOOK_FLOOR_BZZ=0.5

# Optional. Where added stack versions are checked out and built, one directory
# per version and about a gigabyte each. A sibling of the data root, outside the
# tree deploy/deploy.sh rsyncs with --delete, and bind-mounted into the api
# container at this same absolute path.
# Default: /opt/streaming/streaming-infra-manager-versions
STACK_VERSIONS_ROOT=

# Optional. The repositories stack versions are built from, an allow-list.
# Comma separated, each an https GitHub clone address with `#<folder>` where the
# stack sits in it, `.` for the whole tree. An address without a folder is laid
# out like this repository, apps/hls-stream. The first one is where a new
# version and the bundled one are fetched from, and deploy/deploy.sh checks it
# answers without a login. A fork lists its own repository first. Keep the
# upstream ones listed while a version still names them, or it cannot rebuild.
# Default: https://github.com/Solar-Punk-Ltd/streaming-monorepo.git#apps/hls-stream,https://github.com/Solar-Punk-Ltd/swarm-hls-stream.git#.
STACK_SOURCES=

# Optional. Containers on the host's network that the manager may count as
# known, by compose project, with the ports each holds. Docker reports no port
# map for such a container, so without this one running blocks every removal,
# port handover and firewall export on that host. The host's edge reverse
# proxy is the usual case. Projects separated by ';', each
# <project>=<port>/<tcp|udp>,... A project not named here, or a host-network
# container with no project, still blocks as before.
# The trust is per compose project: every host-network container in a named
# project counts as holding only the ports listed for it. So list every port
# those containers hold, because a port left out counts as free and can be
# handed to a deployment.
# A deployment's compose project is its name, so no deployment may carry the
# name of a project named here. Creating one is refused, and an existing one
# stops the manager at startup.
# A malformed value stops the manager at startup. Applies to every host the
# manager reads ports on.
# Example: KNOWN_HOST_NETWORK_PORTS=edge=80/tcp,443/tcp,443/udp
# Default: none.
KNOWN_HOST_NETWORK_PORTS=

# Optional. The directory the api container mounts at /root/.ssh, holding the
# deploy key, an ssh_config with a Host block per deploy target alias, and a
# known_hosts the operator seeds with each target's host key (deploy/README.md
# shows the ssh-keyscan step). Target verification runs ssh with
# StrictHostKeyChecking=yes and refuses a key that is not already there. The
# same files then serve deploy.sh's own ssh and rsync. The api image links
# /etc/ssh/ssh_config to the ssh_config in it, because ssh refuses a per-user
# config it does not own.
# Only needed when deployments target other hosts.
# Default: /opt/streaming/manager-ssh
MANAGER_SSH_DIR=

# Optional. Where the resource monitor reads the host's CPU, memory and disk,
# which docker-compose.yml bind-mounts read-only from /proc and / on the host.
# Each falls back to the mount when it exists and to the container's own /proc
# and / when it does not, so a manager run outside Docker reports its own box.
# Defaults: /host/proc, then /proc. And /host/rootfs, then /.
# HOST_PROC=
# HOST_ROOTFS=

# The manager's own folder on the host, where deploy/deploy.sh puts this
# checkout and where docker-compose.yml mounts it. The deploy reads it from the
# file it sends, so a profile's file names the folder on that profile's host.
# The data, the stack versions and the ssh identity default to folders beside it.
# Default: /opt/streaming/streaming-infra-manager
# MANAGER_ROOT=/opt/streaming/streaming-infra-manager
